\begin_layout Standard
If you look at the text of the above joke,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
,
 perhaps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\family typewriter
BANG SORT Forward SPLIT </
\backslash
W*
\backslash
s+
\backslash
W*/> LOWERCASE < Text goes here >
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and put aside its meaning for a moment,
 you’ll see that some words occur more often than others.
 For example,
 we have two ‘a’s;
 two instances of ‘asks’ and ‘did’;
 three ‘for’s,
 four copies of ‘his’ and five of ‘the’.
 Words like ‘practitioner’ and ‘expressed’ are all alone,
 as are ‘thorns’ and ‘gentleman’.
 But we do have two roses and two breakfasts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It’s a strange observation that in larger bodies of text from many natural languages,
 the most commonly used word occurs about twice as often as the next,
 three times as common as the third,
 and so on.
 We’re getting hints of this here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You may even have bumped into 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Zipf’s law"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zipf's_law"
literal "false"

\end_inset

—
if you rank words,
 sorting by the frequency f with which they are used in written texts,
 then for a given rank r:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Eqn
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\propto\frac{1}{r}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Linguists being what they are,
 some have fancied this up a bit along the lines of:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Eqn
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\propto\frac{1}{\left(r+b\right)^{a}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 is about 1 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is about 2.7.
 Needless to say,
 the idea predates the American linguist George 
\series bold
Kingsley Zipf
\series default
 by many years,
 but he popularised it,
 and his name
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Pronounced ‘Ziff’.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 was strange enough to be sticky.
 There has been a lot of speculation about how this ‘law’ comes about,
 with several possible explanations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Successful words become more popular.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
People are just lazy (“The principle of least effort”).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Stochastic monkeys banging on keyboards to make words at random
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
With a fixed probability of hitting each letter (or a space).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 may generate words following Zipf’s law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
More technically still,
 Zipf’s law is a ‘power law’ 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "related to the Pareto distribution"
target "https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~bart/736/papers/PowerLawTutorial.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Brevity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But there’s another,
 similar law in linguistics.
 If you look back at the words at the start,
 shorter words generally seem to be used more often.
 This is sometimes stretched out to “Zipf’s law of abbreviation”—
but more commonly it’s just the ‘
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "brevity law"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brevity_law"
literal "false"

\end_inset

’,
 a more concise name :)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This does seem to work with natural language,
 but can we use it to good effect when building computer languages?
 I think so.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
There’s also evidence that lexical token usage in Java,
 C and C++ 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "follow similar laws"
target "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306457309000119"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Oral language 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "has also been explored"
target "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6731709/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 (in people from central Ohio),
 even in 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "spoken dialogues"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 If you end up playing with my esolang 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Zyzyxia"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/p/the-naming"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the built-in COMMANDs should make this obvious.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A commonly-used command is likely short.
 For example,
 to increment a value I use UP rather than INC or INCREMENT;
 decrement is DN.
 I chose LEN over LENGTH.
 Some commands are deliberately longer:
 PRECISION,
 PASSWORD,
 UPPERCASE and IMMORTALIZE should therefore make you think before you use them.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We naturally can’t say how people will use a general purpose language,
 so you might find engineers mildly cursing ARCCOS,
 and those doing time-zone arithmetic sweating over JULIAN.
 Like everything else,
 balance is a subjective concept that can start wars.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I’ve also tried to use this idea subtly elsewhere.
 In modern programming,
 it’s conventional to refer to boolean values as “true” or “false”
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Or ‘True’ and ‘False’;
 or ‘TRUE’ and ‘FALSE’,
 and so on.
\end_layout

\end_inset

—
but I spent quite some time exploring alternatives,
 and settled on Yes and No.
 Less typing.
\end_layout

