\begin_layout Section*
Hungarian notation,
 anyone?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Putting aside Monty Python,
 ‘Hungarian notation’ is the practice of naming variables based on their type.
 It comes from the US-centric belief that Hungarians are a bit weird because they put their surname before their forename.
 Hungarian notation does look weird,
 though.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Examples might include iLength for an integer,
 strName for a string,
 pFoo for a pointer,
 u32Danger for an unsigned 32-bit integer,
 and so on.
 It was extensively used by 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Simonyi Károly"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Simonyi"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the Hungarian software developer who moved from Xerox PARC to become Chief Architect at Microsoft.
 AKA 
\series bold
Charles Simonyi
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hungarian notation is now pretty much a legacy,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Microsoft now 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "discourages"
target "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/learnwin32/windows-coding-conventions"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 Hungarian notation;
 but older C/C++ source code in Windows is still infested with it.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 mostly because it’s been discarded in favour of strong typing.
 The machine knows the type,
 and that’s considered good enough.
 Trust the Machine.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This particular transition is however a two-edged sword.
 Early on in our journey,
 we discovered how in C++,
 ‘foo’ 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "might refer to"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/212130688/inscrutable-in-c"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 a variable,
 a function,
 a class,
 a class template,
 a label,
 an enumerator,
 and a lot more.
 Me,
 I think it’s important not only to ensure that something does what it should,
 but also that the label on the tin is distinct and legible.
 Let’s dig a bit deeper.
\end_layout

