\begin_layout Section*
Types and polymorphism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Programmers and many others will be familiar with the concept of polymorphism.
 Despite the length of the name,
 the concept is simple.
 If the ADD command can add integers,
 it makes sense for it also to be able to 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "add two floating point numbers"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/212226868/but"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 without fuss.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
ADD 0.1~ 0.2~
\family default
,
 naturally giving 0.30000000000000004.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 we shouldn’t anticipate surprises with fixed point numbers:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
ADD 0.1 0.2
\family default
,
 producing 0.3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can even extend this idea.
 For example,
 let’s say we are able to make a Button widget and draw it on screen.
 We decide to PUT it somewhere,
 like:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
PUT xCoord yCoord <My button>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But what if we later want to ask a question like “Where is the button?”?
 In early Lisp,
 for example,
 a ‘question’ word would often end in P,
 as in ‘lessp’,
 ‘greaterp’,
 ‘zerop’,
 ‘onep’,
 ‘floatp’ and so on.
 In 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
,
 we might extend our polymorphism:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
PUT * <My widget>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can take ‘*’ as being equivalent to a question like ‘Where?’ or ‘What?’
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The attentive reader will work out that the question returns two coordinates.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 if we want to know whether the HELP command exists,
 we might say 
\family typewriter
COMMAND * <HELP>
\family default
 —
 which returns the value Yes.
 We can even be more emphatic.
 When we say 
\family typewriter
SORT Forward COMMAND * *
\family default
 here’s what we get
