 “What??” Many of those commands will themselves have * options.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
“We’re all consenting adults here”
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation
“Rules are for the obedience of fools and the guidance of wise men”—

\series bold
Harry Day
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The title of this section comes from Python’s originator (and BDFL),
 
\series bold
Guido van Rossum
\series default
,
 who pointed out that it’s often smarter to have sensible community behaviour than formidable restrictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We find a hint of this principle in many languages.
 Python has no constants,
 but something like FOO_VALUE is clearly a constant,
 by convention alone.
 In Common Lisp there’s a 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "bunch of sensible naming conventions"
target "https://llthw.common-lisp.dev/1-01-07-style-guide.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 for example special global variables are signified with ‘earmuffs’,
 for example *package* and *print-base*.
 Ruby uses an exclamation mark to end off a dangerous method.
 Rust 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "distinguishes between"
target "https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/naming.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 UpperCamelCase for Types and Traits,
 and snake_case for many other things.
\end_layout

