\begin_layout Section*
A step too far?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But at first glance,
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
 seems to be flagrantly ignoring Guido.
 We have hard rules.
 COMMANDs are uppercase;
 verbs are lowercase;
 _adverbs_ start and end in an underscore,
 and ‘local’ variables are parochialVariables in camelCase.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Actually,
 all we’ve done is build a solid spine.
 That doesn’t mean our language has to have ankylosing spondylitis,
 though—
a condition where the spine fuses into a rigid,
 inflexible,
 injury-prone rod.
 Consenting adults can also \SpecialChar ldots

\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
SAY <pretty much anything they want (☠!
 
\backslash
128369 ) between 
\family sans
😊
\family typewriter
 a pair of chevrons.>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And it turns out that,
 for example,
 widget names are really just strings in <chevrons>.
 We can be flexible,
 without going crazy.
 Consider \SpecialChar ldots

\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Modules
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every language needs some way of extending itself—
and importing useful things.
 Object-oriented languages often inherit from constructs that are meant to be reusable;
 functional languages concentrate on functions,
 which really are reusable.
 Some languages have #include statements.
 Others say ‘load’,
 ‘import’,
 ‘using’,
 or ‘require’,
 combined with macros,
 patching and a lot more.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
 has modules.
 Like this:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
MODULE <normal>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Generally,
 modules set verbs.
 Here’s where our exploration of Zipf’s various laws takes hold.
 If you look at the normal module,
 it’s littered with short names,
 like setverb,
 self,
 isnoun,
 help,
 pair and triple.
 These are actually enormously useful,
 if used wisely.
 For example,
 pair joins two strings together,
 with a space between.
 You can work out what triple and quad do :)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But here’s the thing.
 In 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
 we have the WORM rule—

\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Write Once Read Many"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/219692586/1-the-worst-decision"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We simply cannot re-define a Noun,
 or a verb.
 If you try this,
 you’ll force an error.
 What does this imply?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Responsibility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are all responsible for what we make,
 to some degree.
 It’s nice,
 convenient and lazy to choose short names.
 But let’s face it—
by Zipf’s law,
 only a small proportion of words will be used often.
 Language doesn’t work any other way,
 it seems,
 whether it’s ‘natural’ or ‘artificial’.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every non-trivial act of programming in some way extends the language.
 It’s possible that in due course,
 some of our creations will achieve general use,
 but most just won’t.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let’s be honest.
 Most entire languages don’t expand the programming space that much!
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 If we choose short names from the start,
 we encroach on the programming space and invite collisions.
 Rather be conservative.
 Let’s turn this into a strong principle,
 for the guidance of the wise:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\emph on
Keep your names long.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I often find that when I’m programming,
 certain patterns recur again and again.
 Over time,
 these may become candidates for general use,
 perhaps with short-ish names.
 But to start,
 it may be smarter to name our verbs along these lines:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
SET <tlc_specific_verb> < \SpecialChar ldots
 my code here \SpecialChar ldots
 >
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\SpecialChar ldots
 where ‘tlc’ says which module the verb belongs to.
 Just make sure that the label says what’s on the can.
 I think that’s a good programming principle,
 whatever language you’re programming in.
 Would that it were more respected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One more thing.
 Wouldn’t it be nice if we could be sure that something like
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
<my_verb>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\SpecialChar ldots
 didn’t ever appear in a module?
 Not as a rule,
 but in the consenting adult sense,
 a strong guideline.
 This would mean that you could freely use <my_ without ever worrying about a collision.
 Wouldn’t that be nice?
\end_layout

